// design 229 x design 80, the SOUNDNESS property between them: a `public
// import` RE-EXPORT does not widen a `public(package)` symbol. The facade
// republishes the NAME; it cannot republish the TIER.
//
// `pkgvis`'s lib.saw says `public import pkgvis.secret.{pkg_secret}` — a real
// attempt at the widening — while `pkg_secret` itself is `public(package)` in
// `pkgvis.secret`. This entry file is outside the package, so reaching it
// THROUGH the facade must be refused exactly as reaching it directly is
// (`module_path_package_visibility_error.saw` pins the direct route).
//
// WHY THIS PIN EARNS ITS PLACE (Aug 20, the kcore re-narrowing): kcore's
// lib.saw `public import`s eleven names out of a package whose other 108 are
// now `public(package)`. A widening re-export means one facade line silently
// republishes whatever it names, and design 80's gate protects nothing — while
// every test still passes, because nothing fails. kcore is not exploited today
// only because its facade happens to name eleven genuinely-`public` symbols;
// that is an accident of this pass, not a guarantee.
//
// `export229_scoped_import_error.saw` pins the neighbouring rule
// (`public(package) import` is refused outright). This pins the one that has
// no diagnostic of its own to notice — which is why it went unnoticed.
// XFAIL: DF-232j a `public import` re-export WIDENS a `public(package)` symbol
// to the world — design 229's facade bypasses design 80's package tier. This
// file compiles and prints 7 today; the EXPECT below states the intent.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: `pkg_secret` is public(package) in `pkgvis.secret`
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --module-path pkgvis={TESTDIR}/modules/pkgvis232f
import pkgvis

func main() {
    print(pkgvis.pkg_secret())
}
